{"type":"assistant","message":{"type":"message","model":"kimi-k3","usage":{"input_tokens":39659,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_5_m_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_1_h_tokens":0},"role":"assistant","id":"02196ae57fdf0a7a49612c740a6b0dd4","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now batch 2: ProductCard component, CategoryTile (Explore), CategoryChip (Home groceries), NavBar component with 5 tabs, header component maybe. \n\nFor NavBar: I'll build as a component \"NavBar/Shop\" etc? Simpler: build one NavBar component with a variant per current tab? Component sets with variants complicate instancing with text overrides. Simpler: create NavBar as a component built per-screen inline but reuse tab item sub-components? For component reuse credit, make \"NavTab\" component (icon + label) and a \"NavBar\" component that contains 5 NavTab instances; then per-screen instance overrides the active color. Overrides on instance children work fine (fills of text/icon instances). Icons are component instances inside; to change their color I'd need to override the nested vector fills... The media icons are components with vector children; overriding fill on nested instance requires setting fills on the instance itself — instance fill override applies? Setting instance.fills doesn't exist; but you can set fills on children of instances? Actually you CAN modify fills of nodes inside an instance (they are overridable). E.g., inst.children[0].fills works.\n\nSimpler approach: NavBar component = white bg + top border + 5 NavTab instances. NavTab component = frame 79x56 with icon instance + label text, default state (grey). For active tab in each screen, override the icon's vector fill and label color to green. I'll write a helper function `setTab(navInstance, idx)` that recolors.\n\nTo recolor icon: icon instance children are vectors/groups; set fills of all descendants. I'll implement recursive paint.\n\nProductCard