{"type":"assistant","message":{"type":"message","model":"kimi-k3","usage":{"input_tokens":41832,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_5_m_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_1_h_tokens":0},"role":"assistant","id":"02106ae580220a7ae814f95de24bc214","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now create the NavBar component with variant-like states — I'll create one NavBar component (default = Shop active) and instances will swap icon colors. Actually simpler: create a single NavBar component with \"Shop\" current; for other tabs I'll create instances and override colors. Icon colors inside instances can be overridden by finding vectors. But icons are instances inside; recoloring nested instance children is possible via findAll and setting fills — overrides allowed on instances. Simpler approach: create 4 NavBar components (Shop, Explore, Cart, Account)? The brief wants reusable components; variants are nice but a set of states as separate components is fine too (\"Design all other states... as reusable components\"). To keep it manageable, I'll build one NavBar component with variant property via ComponentSet: variants Shop/Explore/Cart/Account. Variants are the cleanest.\n\nLet me create a ComponentSet \"NavBar\" with 4 variants. Each variant: 5 tabs (Shop, Explore, Cart, Favourite, Account) each with icon instance + label; active tab icon+label green, others grey/ink. Container: white bg, top shadow, radius 15.\n\nBuilding a component set: create components named \"NavBar/Tab=Shop\" etc., then figma.combineAsVariants. The names use commas: \"Tab=Shop\". Combine into \"NavBar\".\n\nEach variant 414x84. Layout: bar bg white 414x84, top border shadow. 5 tabs at width 414/5=82.8 each, icon 24 + label 12.\n\nAlso I need instances of icons: Icon_Shop 4:163, Icon_Explore 4:130, Icon_Cart 4:121, Icon_Favourite 4:136, Icon_Account 4:118.\n\nFor active state, I want the icon to appear green. The icon components' internal vector colors — I can override instance children fills. When I create an instance of Icon_Shop, I can find the vector child and set fills to green. Icon instances have children (vectors). Let me check their structure quickly as part of the build and override.\n\nAlternative simpler: wrap each icon instance, and for the active tab draw a green dot under the label + green text, leaving icons dark. Many real apps do that. But nicer to recolor icon. I'll try recoloring nested: after creating instance, instance.findAll(n => n.type===\"VECTOR\" || ...) and set fills. Overrides on nested children of instances ARE supported.\n\nLet me build NavBar variants.","signature":""}],"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06ae3923-d12a-4248-9468-ebfe96d71820","uuid":"54e6726a-2186-4ccb-869e-d4999628dfdb","timestamp":"2026-10-31T05:44:17.788Z","thinking_duration_ms":11716}
